# {{ name | kebab_case }}

A TanStack Start BFF generated by the `tanstack` Moon template.

## ZITADEL Authentication

Authentication is built into this template and runs in the TanStack Start
server runtime. The home page is public; `/dashboard` requires a signed-in
user. The app uses OpenID Connect Authorization Code with PKCE. The browser
receives only an opaque, HTTP-only session handle; the user's minimal identity
and OAuth access/refresh tokens stay in Redis for at most eight hours. Logout
removes the Redis session and attempts to revoke its refresh token before
redirecting through ZITADEL's end-session endpoint. Server code can call the
configured Axum API with a bearer token using `fetchAuthenticatedApi` from
`src/server/api.server.ts`. Tokens are never returned to browser components.
The user's name and email come from the ID token when ZITADEL includes them,
and otherwise from the userinfo endpoint.
The flow follows ZITADEL's
[Authorization Code + PKCE guide](https://zitadel.com/docs/guides/integrate/login/oidc/login-users).

### Local setup

From the repository root, run:

```bash
bun run setup
moon run {{ name | kebab_case }}:dev
```

`bun run setup` starts ZITADEL and Redis, creates this app's ZITADEL Web
application (Authorization Code with PKCE, the **Refresh Token** grant, and
Development Mode for `http://localhost:{{ port }}`), and fills in this app's
`.env`: `ZITADEL_CLIENT_ID`, a `SESSION_SECRET`, and `API_BASE_URL` when the
workspace has one Axum API. Then use **Log in** in the header. `/dashboard`
makes a server-to-server `GET /api/me` call when `API_BASE_URL` is set; it never
forwards the browser's cookie to the API. To add an API, generate one with
`moon generate axum -- --name api --port 4000` and run `bun run setup` again.

To set it up by hand instead, create a Web application in the ZITADEL Console
with Authorization Code and PKCE, enable the **Refresh Token** grant, and
register these URLs, with Development Mode on for plain HTTP:

- Redirect URI: `http://localhost:{{ port }}/auth/callback`
- Post-logout redirect URI: `http://localhost:{{ port }}/auth/logout/callback`

Then copy `.env.example` to `.env` and set `ZITADEL_CLIENT_ID` and a
`SESSION_SECRET` (`openssl rand -base64 32`), and set the shared
`ZITADEL_PROJECT_ID` in the repository root `.env`. The app requests
`offline_access` and the project audience scope, so every API in the same
ZITADEL project accepts its access tokens.

This template requires a server runtime; a static export cannot handle OIDC
callbacks or sessions.

### Production checklist

- Register a separate ZITADEL Web application for each environment: Authorization
  Code with PKCE (no client secret), the **Refresh Token** grant, and HTTPS
  redirect and post-logout URLs. Leave **Development Mode** off; it exists only
  to allow `http://localhost` URLs.
- Set `APP_URL` and `ZITADEL_ISSUER` (and `API_BASE_URL`) to HTTPS origins. The
  app refuses plain HTTP outside local development. Behind a proxy, forward the
  original `Host` and `Origin` headers: logout compares the request `Origin` to
  `APP_URL`.
- Point `REDIS_URL` at a Redis that requires authentication and TLS
  (`rediss://user:password@host:6379`). Session keys are namespaced per app.
- Generate a different `SESSION_SECRET` for each environment
  (`openssl rand -base64 32`) and keep it in your secret manager. It only seals
  the short-lived sign-in cookie, so rotating it interrupts in-flight sign-ins
  but not signed-in users.
- Signed-in sessions live in Redis for at most eight hours. They carry a schema
  version (`SESSION_VERSION` in `src/server/session.server.ts`); after a release
  changes the stored shape, bump it and users simply sign in again.
- Name and email are read from the ID token, or from the userinfo endpoint when
  the ID token omits them. Enabling **Include user's profile info in the ID
  Token** on the ZITADEL application skips that extra request but is optional.
- Failed sign-in, sign-out, token-refresh, and API steps are written to the
  server log as `[auth] ...` lines with the error name and code, never tokens or
  cookies. Send server logs somewhere you can search.
- The OIDC discovery document is cached for an hour. If refreshing it fails, the
  last good copy keeps being used, so a brief ZITADEL outage does not fail every
  sign-in.

Login always ships with generated projects, whether or not demo routes are
included.

# Building For Production

```bash
moon run {{ name | kebab_case }}:build    # build into dist/
bun run start                           # serve dist/ (reads PORT from .env)
moon run {{ name | kebab_case }}:docker   # build the production image
```

`bun run start` runs `scripts/serve.ts`, which serves the client assets and
hands every other request to the server build. `vite build` bundles the
server's npm dependencies into `dist/`, so the image carries no `node_modules`.
It runs the same server on port 3000 and reads the settings from `.env.example` plus the shared
`ZITADEL_ISSUER`, `ZITADEL_PROJECT_ID`, and `REDIS_URL`. The repository's
`deploy/` folder runs it behind HTTPS.

## Styling

This project uses [Tailwind CSS](https://tailwindcss.com/) for styling.

### Removing Tailwind CSS

If you prefer not to use Tailwind CSS:

1. Remove the demo pages in `src/routes/demo/`
2. Replace the Tailwind import in `src/styles.css` with your own styles
3. Remove `tailwindcss()` from the plugins array in `vite.config.ts`
4. Remove `@tailwindcss/vite` and `tailwindcss` from `package.json`

## Linting & Formatting

This project uses [Biome](https://biomejs.dev/) for linting and formatting. The following scripts are available:


```bash
bun run lint
bun run format
bun run check
bun run test
```


## Shadcn

Shared components and design tokens are provided by `@vern/ui`. Import them from the package:

```tsx
import { Button } from "@vern/ui/components/button";
```

From this app's directory, the CLI routes shared primitives into
`packages/ui` through the workspace configuration:

```bash
cd apps/{{ name | kebab_case }}
bunx --bun shadcn@latest add card
```



## Routing

This project uses [TanStack Router](https://tanstack.com/router) with file-based routing. Routes are managed as files in `src/routes`.

### Adding A Route

To add a new route to your application just add a new file in the `./src/routes` directory.

TanStack will automatically generate the content of the route file for you.

Now that you have two routes you can use a `Link` component to navigate between them.

### Adding Links

To use SPA (Single Page Application) navigation you will need to import the `Link` component from `@tanstack/react-router`.

```tsx
import { Link } from "@tanstack/react-router";
```

Then anywhere in your JSX you can use it like so:

```tsx
<Link to="/about">About</Link>
```

This will create a link that will navigate to the `/about` route.

More information on the `Link` component can be found in the [Link documentation](https://tanstack.com/router/v1/docs/framework/react/api/router/linkComponent).

### Using A Layout

In the File Based Routing setup the layout is located in `src/routes/__root.tsx`. Anything you add to the root route will appear in all the routes. The route content will appear in the JSX where you render `{children}` in the `shellComponent`.

Here is an example layout that includes a header:

```tsx
import { HeadContent, Scripts, createRootRoute } from '@tanstack/react-router'

export const Route = createRootRoute({
  head: () => ({
    meta: [
      { charSet: 'utf-8' },
      { name: 'viewport', content: 'width=device-width, initial-scale=1' },
      { title: 'My App' },
    ],
  }),
  shellComponent: ({ children }) => (
    <html lang="en">
      <head>
        <HeadContent />
      </head>
      <body>
        <header>
          <nav>
            <Link to="/">Home</Link>
            <Link to="/about">About</Link>
          </nav>
        </header>
        {children}
        <Scripts />
      </body>
    </html>
  ),
})
```

More information on layouts can be found in the [Layouts documentation](https://tanstack.com/router/latest/docs/framework/react/guide/routing-concepts#layouts).

## Server Functions

TanStack Start provides server functions that allow you to write server-side code that seamlessly integrates with your client components.

```tsx
import { createServerFn } from '@tanstack/react-start'

const getServerTime = createServerFn({
  method: 'GET',
}).handler(async () => {
  return new Date().toISOString()
})

// Use in a component
function MyComponent() {
  const [time, setTime] = useState('')
  
  useEffect(() => {
    getServerTime().then(setTime)
  }, [])
  
  return <div>Server time: {time}</div>
}
```

## API Routes

You can create API routes by using the `server` property in your route definitions:

```tsx
import { createFileRoute } from '@tanstack/react-router'
import { json } from '@tanstack/react-start'

export const Route = createFileRoute('/api/hello')({
  server: {
    handlers: {
      GET: () => json({ message: 'Hello, World!' }),
    },
  },
})
```

## Data Fetching

There are multiple ways to fetch data in your application. You can use TanStack Query to fetch data from a server. But you can also use the `loader` functionality built into TanStack Router to load the data for a route before it's rendered.

For example:

```tsx
import { createFileRoute } from '@tanstack/react-router'

export const Route = createFileRoute('/people')({
  loader: async () => {
    const response = await fetch('https://swapi.dev/api/people')
    return response.json()
  },
  component: PeopleComponent,
})

function PeopleComponent() {
  const data = Route.useLoaderData()
  return (
    <ul>
      {data.results.map((person) => (
        <li key={person.name}>{person.name}</li>
      ))}
    </ul>
  )
}
```

Loaders simplify your data fetching logic dramatically. Check out more information in the [Loader documentation](https://tanstack.com/router/latest/docs/framework/react/guide/data-loading#loader-parameters).


